(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-exam-1-master-exam-form-j"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form J","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which antidote reactivates acetylcholinesterase after insecticide poisoning?","choices":["Atropine","Pralidoxime","Dantrolene","Physostigmine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, though it does not penetrate the central nervous system. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Where does amphotericin B come from?","choices":["A Penicillium mould","A synthetic triazole scaffold","Bacillus polymyxa fermentation","A soil Streptomycetaceae organism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it forms channels in ergosterol-containing membranes. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Which properties do the catecholamines share?","choices":["Low potency and a long half-life","High potency and rapid inactivation","Good oral absorption and slow clearance","Resistance to monoamine oxidase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, along with poor penetration into the brain. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Clindamycin is the drug of choice for which problems?","choices":["Urinary tract infection and pyelonephritis","Nosocomial pneumonia and ventilator pneumonia","Toxin-mediated disease and osteomyelitis","Meningitis requiring central nervous system entry"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, along with skin infection and surgical prophylaxis in allergy. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"How does levofloxacin differ from ciprofloxacin?","choices":["Improved Gram-negative activity but no atypicals","Improved Gram-positive activity and Pseudomonas cover","Loss of Pseudomonas coverage entirely","A shift from oral to intravenous only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which supports its use in community-acquired pneumonia. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Why is propranolol useful in hyperthyroidism?","choices":["It reduces thyroid hormone synthesis","It blocks hormone release from the gland","It blunts widespread sympathetic stimulation","It speeds hormone clearance from plasma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, treating the symptoms rather than the thyroid. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"What do the ganglionic blockers act on?","choices":["Nicotinic receptors of both autonomic ganglia","Muscarinic receptors of the parasympathetic system","Nicotinic receptors at the neuromuscular junction","Alpha and beta adrenergic receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, blocking the entire output of the autonomic system. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What is distinctive about bethanechol's receptor activity?","choices":["Strong nicotinic activity with no muscarinic action","Equal muscarinic and nicotinic activity","Strong muscarinic activity with no nicotinic action","Activity only at the neuromuscular junction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it resists acetylcholinesterase. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What does epinephrine do to systolic and diastolic pressures?","choices":["Raises both systolic and diastolic","Lowers both systolic and diastolic","Lowers systolic and raises diastolic","Raises systolic and lowers diastolic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside increased cardiac output. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"What is the depot effect in skin?","choices":["The drug is metabolised within the epidermis","The vehicle evaporates and concentrates the drug","Skin acts as a reservoir for the drug","Occlusion drives the drug into the bloodstream"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which can permit once-daily dosing of short-acting drugs. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What did voriconazole replace for systemic aspergillosis?","choices":["Fluconazole","Amphotericin B","Caspofungin","Griseofulvin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, following its introduction in 2002. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which topical antifungal has no activity against Candida?","choices":["Nystatin","Clotrimazole","Tolnaftate","Miconazole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, whereas nystatin is used precisely for candidal infection. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What form of drug is oseltamivir given as?","choices":["An already active guanine analogue","A live attenuated preparation","A prodrug converted to the carboxylate","An irreversible enzyme inhibitor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and resistance is becoming a problem over time. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"How many sequential steps make up cholinergic neurotransmission?","choices":["Four","Five","Six","Eight"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, running from synthesis through to recycling of choline. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How does amphetamine act on the adrenergic nerve?","choices":["It binds postsynaptic receptors directly","It inhibits monoamine oxidase in the gut","It depletes vesicular stores permanently","It blocks reuptake and releases catecholamines"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, raising blood pressure and cardiac stimulation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Which central nervous system effects follow acyclovir?","choices":["Depression and vivid nightmares","Seizures, delirium and tremor","Short-term memory blockade","Mental confusion and blurred vision"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside bone marrow suppression. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"What is phenoxybenzamine used to treat?","choices":["Benign prostatic hyperplasia","Phaeochromocytoma","Chronic open-angle glaucoma","Hypertensive emergency by infusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, along with autonomic hyperreflexia in paraplegic patients. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which fluoroquinolone needs no dosing consideration?","choices":["Moxifloxacin","Levofloxacin","Ciprofloxacin","All three require renal adjustment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, while ciprofloxacin and levofloxacin are renally adjusted. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"What is the advantage of topical antibiotics in acne?","choices":["They lack systemic side effects","They work faster than any oral agent","They avoid bacterial resistance entirely","They can be used in pregnancy safely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is what separates them from oral antibiotics. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Why is resistance to vancomycin harder to develop?","choices":["It binds the substrate rather than the enzyme","It is given only by the intravenous route","It is bacteriostatic rather than bactericidal","It is always combined with a second agent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, so a single enzymatic change does not defeat it. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"How often should a patient with acne wash?","choices":["Twice a day","Every two hours while awake","Once a week with a strong soap","Only when the skin feels oily"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because over-washing causes drying and irritation. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What makes up the atopic triad with eczema?","choices":["Psoriasis and vitiligo","Chronic urticaria and angio-oedema","Asthma and allergic rhinoconjunctivitis","Acne and rosacea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with skin barrier dysfunction central to the disease. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Why is acyclovir selective for infected cells?","choices":["It is only absorbed at sites of infection","Human cells lack the receptor for it","Viral thymidine kinase phosphorylates it","It binds viral capsid protein directly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, so activation happens mainly inside infected cells. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"How is oral ketoconazole taken?","choices":["With meals","On an empty stomach","With a high-fat meal specifically","Separated from calcium and iron"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is also available in topical form. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"What does the degree of inflammation from imiquimod indicate?","choices":["It signals an allergic reaction to stop","It has no relationship to the response","It means the dose is too high","It parallels the efficacy of treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, so irritation is expected in virtually all patients. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which drugs inhibit propranolol metabolism?","choices":["Barbiturates, phenytoin and rifampin together","Iron, antacids and calcium products","Tyramine-containing fermented foods","Cimetidine, fluoxetine, paroxetine and ritonavir"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, while barbiturates, phenytoin and rifampin induce it. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"Which topical antibiotic is preferred for acne?","choices":["Erythromycin","Mupirocin","Bacitracin","Clindamycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, while erythromycin is losing efficacy to resistance. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What do the gut and bladder do in anticholinergic toxicity?","choices":["Increase tone, causing diarrhoea and urgency","Lose tone, causing constipation and retention","Are unaffected by these drugs","Empty rapidly and completely"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, while the heart runs alone with tachycardia. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How is griseofulvin absorption improved?","choices":["Taking it with a high-fat meal","Taking it on an empty stomach","Avoiding calcium and iron products","Taking it with an acidic drink"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because the drug is very lipid soluble. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"At a creatinine clearance above 70 millilitres per minute, how often is vancomycin given?","choices":["Every 8 hours","Every 12 hours","Every 24 hours","One dose, then check a random level"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The interval lengthens as clearance falls. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What cardiovascular reflex do the alpha blockers induce?","choices":["Reflex bradycardia","Reflex tachycardia","No change in heart rate","Reflex bronchodilation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the effects are more profound on standing. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which agents are indirect-acting adrenergic agonists?","choices":["Ephedrine and pseudoephedrine","Epinephrine and norepinephrine","Phenylephrine and isoproterenol","Amphetamine, cocaine and tyramine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, while ephedrine and pseudoephedrine are mixed-action. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Which anticholinergic sign is described as blind as a bat?","choices":["Cutaneous flushing and redness","Mydriasis with blurry vision","Hyperthermia","Dry mucous membranes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, as one of the classic toxidrome descriptions. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which antimuscarinic treats excessive sweating and drooling?","choices":["Ipratropium","Tiotropium","Scopolamine","Glycopyrrolate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it also reduces preoperative secretions. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which musculoskeletal injury do the fluoroquinolones cause?","choices":["Tendonitis and Achilles tendon rupture","Rhabdomyolysis with a raised creatine kinase","Depression of skeletal growth in children","Malignant hyperthermia under anaesthesia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with caution advised below 18 years of age. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which macrolide treats Chlamydia trachomatis conjunctivitis or pneumonia in an infant?","choices":["Azithromycin","Clarithromycin","Erythromycin","Telithromycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, while azithromycin covers Chlamydia trachomatis more generally. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which infection does tolnaftate fail to treat?","choices":["Tinea unguium","Tinea pedis","Tinea cruris","Tinea corporis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, though it works for pedis, cruris and corporis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"Why does nystatin have limited systemic side effects?","choices":["It is rapidly cleared by the kidney","It binds plasma proteins almost completely","It is poorly absorbed","It is inactivated by stomach acid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, so it is used topically and for oral candidiasis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Which sites absorb topical drugs most readily?","choices":["Scrotum, face, axilla and scalp","Palms, soles and the thick skin of the back","The shins and the dorsum of the foot","Skin over the knees and elbows"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why potency is matched to the site treated. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How does pyrantel pamoate paralyse a worm?","choices":["It blocks microtubule assembly","It acts as a depolarising neuromuscular blocker","It competitively blocks nicotinic receptors","It starves the parasite of glucose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, by releasing acetylcholine and inhibiting cholinesterase. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"What loading dose is used for vancomycin in critically ill patients?","choices":["15 to 20 milligrams per kilogram","25 to 30 milligrams per kilogram","7 milligrams per kilogram once daily","4 to 6 milligrams per kilogram daily"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, rounded to the nearest 500 milligrams. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What does nicotine do at high concentrations?","choices":["Stimulates the receptor more strongly","Has no effect on the receptor","Blocks the receptor","Converts the receptor to a muscarinic type"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, whereas at low concentrations it stimulates it. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which are major indicators of atopic dermatitis?","choices":["Raised immunoglobulin E and positive skin tests","Dry patchy scales and skin infection susceptibility","Pruritus, typical rash sites, chronicity, family history","Pustules on the trunk and comedones"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, as against minor indicators such as raised immunoglobulin E. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Why does acyclovir stop viral DNA elongation?","choices":["It cross-links the two DNA strands","It inhibits viral DNA polymerase allosterically","It prevents the virus uncoating","It lacks the sugar moiety of a guanine nucleotide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, so the chain cannot be extended once it is added. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"Why did cisatracurium replace atracurium?","choices":["It works considerably faster","It does not require reversal","It can be given orally","It has fewer adverse effects"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, being the isomer of the parent drug. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Why can aztreonam be used in true penicillin allergy?","choices":["It is not a beta-lactam antibiotic at all","It has no cross-reactivity with beta-lactams","It is always co-administered with a steroid","Its allergic reactions are limited to rash"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is the practical reason it is kept in reserve. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which cephalosporins carry antipseudomonal activity?","choices":["Cefazolin, cephalexin and cefprozil","Ceftriaxone, cefotaxime and cefdinir","Ceftaroline, cefoxitin and cefotetan","Ceftazidime, cefepime and ceftolozane"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Most other cephalosporins lack it. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which enzymes inactivate the catecholamines?","choices":["Acetylcholinesterase and pseudocholinesterase","Dihydrofolate reductase and thymidylate synthase","Catechol-O-methyltransferase and monoamine oxidase","Cytochrome P450 3A4 and 1A2"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, acting postsynaptically and within the neuron. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What is ceftolozane/tazobactam approved to treat?","choices":["Complicated intra-abdominal and urinary infection","Community-acquired pneumonia and skin infection","Neutropenic fever and ventilator pneumonia","Meningitis and other central nervous system infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with metronidazole added for the intra-abdominal indication. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is carbachol used for clinically?","choices":["Stimulating an atonic bladder","Diagnosing myasthenia gravis","Reversing anticholinergic overdose","Lowering intraocular pressure in glaucoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, causing miosis and spasm of accommodation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"How does valacyclovir improve on acyclovir?","choices":["Bioavailability rises from 22 to 70 percent","It covers cytomegalovirus as well","It can be given topically to the eye","It avoids renal excretion entirely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because valacyclovir is a prodrug. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"How does imiquimod work?","choices":["It stimulates interferon and tumour necrosis factor release","It is a guanine analogue incorporated into viral DNA","It inhibits viral neuraminidase","It prevents the virus attaching to the cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, acting as an immunomodulator rather than a direct antiviral. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"How is malignant hyperthermia treated?","choices":["Rapid cooling and pralidoxime","Rapid cooling and dantrolene","Warming and intravenous atropine","Neostigmine to reverse the blockade"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, since dantrolene blocks calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"How does bacitracin work?","choices":["It binds bacterial transfer RNA","It interrupts the cytoplasmic membrane","It inhibits bacterial protein synthesis","It prevents cell wall synthesis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, as a peptide antibiotic with broad coverage. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which cephalosporin generation gains Gram-negative reach without losing Gram-positive?","choices":["Second generation","Third generation","Fourth generation","First generation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is what separates cefepime from the third generation. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"When is a vancomycin trough drawn?","choices":["30 minutes after the infusion finishes","Immediately after the first dose is given","At any point in the dosing interval","15 to 30 minutes before the next dose"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, usually around the third or fourth dose. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which vancomycin indication is given by mouth?","choices":["Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia","Endocarditis in a penicillin-allergic patient","Clostridium difficile infection","Surgical prophylaxis in penicillin allergy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Other indications use the intravenous route. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"How long does griseofulvin treatment of a scalp infection take?","choices":["Six to nine months","About one month","Up to twelve months","Six to twelve weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, against 6 to 9 months for fingernails. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"Why does paralysis last unusually long in some patients given succinylcholine?","choices":["Antibodies to the nicotinic receptor","Accumulation of the drug in keratin","Slow renal clearance of the drug","Genetic pseudocholinesterase deficiency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, since plasma pseudocholinesterase normally clears it fast. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Where does glycine act as an inhibitory neurotransmitter?","choices":["Neurons of the spinal cord","Neurons of the cerebral cortex","The neuromuscular junction","The adrenal medulla"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, as distinct from gamma-aminobutyric acid in the brain. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 16"}]};
